{"id":"standard_explosion","compound_name":"Graphs::Aero::standard_explosion","source_path":"standard_explosion/standard_explosion.json","category":"Fire","author":"Autodesk","description":"A aero and combustion graph producing a standard explosion","documentation_file":"standard_explosion_docs_ENU.md","documentation":"## Standard Explosion Graph\n\n### What it does\n\nThis graph uses aero with combustion to produce a run-of-the-mill-explosion.\n\n### Inputs and outputs \n\nInputs are any geometry from which the explosion will originate, these will emit the fuel. Output is the explosion aero volume\n\n### Useful information\n\n- This graph serves as a great starting point for any typical explosion. It works through the simple concept of emitting fuel along with the air of the source.  If the `temperature` of the source is set to the `ignition temperature` of the fuel, the fuel ignites and an explosive combustion reaction takes place.  The energy of the blast can be adjusted using the fuel `burn rate`.  Different types of fuels will produce different results. \n\n- Scene scale is important, as bigger volumes of fuel will explode more dramatically.  Please set the `scene units in meters` accordingly.   \n\n- Performance is related to the `fluid detail size` on the source air node, and the magnitude of the blast.  Higher `burn rate` and `expansion scale` produce extreme velocities which take longer to compute.","library_dependencies":{"Amino":"2.15.0.9","bif":"2.15.0.9","fields":"2.15.0.9","fluids":"2.15.0.9","geometries":"2.15.0.9","simulation":"2.15.0.9"},"graph_ports":[{"portName":"out_geometry","portDirection":"output","portType":"Object"}],"terminal_states":[{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::final","enabled":"false"},{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::proxy","enabled":"false"},{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::diagnostic","enabled":"false"}],"counts":{"nodes":10,"connections":10,"values":54,"compounds":1},"node_types":{"Simulation::Aero::aero_solver_settings":1,"Simulati